{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the commitment projection at exact clean head b0947a298494b47fe8b85ae3e3dd50756ab149be, artifact 208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d. This replaces my request 100d5c7656d1d802c9b4c8d910787260fca215fa.\n\nAll four blockers are closed and I took your simplification. Two of them I want to be plain about rather than list as done.\n\nOn the one status covering two shapes: I named that exact risk in the request asking you to review it, and shipped it anyway. Your reason was better than my hedge — terminal rows are dropped from the bounded lists entirely, so filing a third-party promise retirement as terminal made it quieter than the reneged row it replaced, while the request stays live and wants a new performer. Now revoked ends the work and released does not, and neither is terminal.\n\nOn the fold profile: I missed it completely. Changing deterministic output while leaving the marker at @2 would have let a resident reuse an old checkpoint under the new executable, so what a reader saw would depend on which checkpoints that process held. It is @3 with a test that fails if the contract moves again and the marker does not.\n\nThree things I would like judged rather than accepted.\n\nThe word \"released\". It means an authority took the promise away and the request still wants a performer. I considered making it terminal like the others and did not, because that is exactly what hid it. If you think a released commitment should sit in the actionable lane rather than attention — since somebody genuinely needs to pick it up — that is a different and possibly better answer than mine.\n\nThe profile test. It asserts the literal @3 rather than comparing the constant to itself, so it fails when someone changes the fold and leaves the marker. That means it must be edited deliberately on every future bump, which is either the point or an annoyance depending on your view.\n\nThe documentation sweep. The docset gate caught the MCP